s have warmed since 1985.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 61\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>61\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 65\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (1989). The low of <strong>43\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Fayetteville has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Fayetteville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Fayetteville has set <strong class=\"sig\">17 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 85 that chance alone would give, and <strong>92 record highs</strong>, close to the 85 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (83). Fayetteville's all-time high is 107\u00b0F, on July 22, 1932.","t_chg":"Fayetteville's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1985.","p_chg":"Across 107 complete years: Fayetteville's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.19\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.29), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1910. Fayetteville's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.12 to +0.07) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.23\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.93 to +1.52) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.76\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.47 to +1.04). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 6 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; January and October fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Fayetteville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.38 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 44.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1936 (69.12 in); the driest, 2007 (29.47 in). Fayetteville almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}